+
+## SMS verification for sign-up
+
+The sign-up flow sends and checks SMS codes through Twilio Verify on the server,
+then calls the existing registration service after approval. Set these runtime
+server environment variables (never expose them with a `PUBLIC_` prefix):
+
+- `TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID`
+- `TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN`
+- `TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID`
+- `TWILIO_VERIFY_TEMPLATE_SID`
+
+Configure the Verify service for six-digit codes with its default 10-minute
+validity. Create an approved custom SMS template supporting a `domain`
+substitution. The actual delivered message must end with the following line,
+using Twilio's code substitution and the supplied domain substitution:
+
+```text
+Your DevTools Times verification code is 012345.
+
+@your-site.example #012345
+```
+
+The last line must contain only the hostname and code, with no scheme, port,
+path, or trailing signature. See [Twilio verification templates](https://www.twilio.com/docs/verify/verification-templates).
+Serve production pages over HTTPS for WebOTP. Unsupported browsers and declined
+permissions retain keyboard autofill, paste, and manual code entry.
+
+The local endpoint uses Astro's server session to bind the phone and verification
+SID, limits retries, and keeps credentials and passcodes out of URLs and logs.
+Configure Twilio Verify fraud protection, geographic permissions, and service rate
+limits before exposing SMS sends publicly. Multi-instance deployments should use
+a shared Astro session storage driver. The external registration service must
+also restrict direct account creation if SMS verification is mandatory for every
+client; this repository cannot enforce policies on that external service.
